#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1260.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065317} :activity/id #uuid "bdb82b74-3fdc-5083-a106-20c7dc1287f8" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050411 :topic/id #uuid "46d83f8d-b8f7-5858-99d2-3e75f2b9bd8c" :topic/math-academy-id 287} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065317 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065384 :tutorial/content "Suppose we're told that the radius of a circle is **growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second**, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27d0543834333202411a9700401b7e33329a255c7103c9f2ebe394d8c1cf694b.png)\n\nIf the radius of the circle is growing, then its area is also increasing. How do we calculate the **rate at which the area is increasing**?\n\nThe rate at which the area is changing can be expressed using a derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is the area of the circle, measured in $\\textrm{cm}^2,$ and $t$ is the time in seconds. \n\nWe know that the area is related to the radius $r$ as\n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the radius of a circle is growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt} = 2 \\, \\textrm{cm}.$ Substituting this information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot 2\\pi r=4\\pi r\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd, that's it, we're done! \n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $r$ at that moment into the above. So, for example, if we want to compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at the moment when $r=6\\,\\textrm{cm},$ we plug this into the above and get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4\\pi \\cdot 6 = 24\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6925e269-127a-5cf1-8116-aa809d1cf42f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 488 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2f58c76e-8d14-575a-b5de-62cc2003eb38" :step/math-academy-id 906 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065319}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065318 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064379 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064023 :question/id #uuid "7aceecc7-33ca-5a7e-a4aa-cf1a313785fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-225"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2dbfe46-55f0-5d6a-b410-164f0aa2345f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064659} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253278} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290446} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369203} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370594} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506535} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506590} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521456} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521552}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "a12978db-32b6-52fc-a426-abfda5e434ae" :step/math-academy-id 907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065320}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065319 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064380 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064024 :question/id #uuid "fba09533-20f9-5e4c-9786-cfbe7b179512" :question/math-academy-id "e-226"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "72b11864-0846-5b22-aa45-5a20079afafb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049225} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049658} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050089}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064821} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250509} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317523} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370582} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371056}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Circle"} :step/id #uuid "9c671f5f-a9e8-5694-8457-ab3f9df8b239" :step/math-academy-id 910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065318}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065320 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064381 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064025 :question/id #uuid "87c5af76-7652-525a-aeb2-d419bab613e0" :question/math-academy-id "e-227"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dabb3127-172e-5a68-8e27-d22057f8ea8e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050966}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064621} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233232} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297169} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297434} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370591} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395790} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506534} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521513}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Related Rate With a Sphere: Word Problem"} :step/id #uuid "318a88e0-e4a9-5b31-a8c3-9cb7712ff53b" :step/math-academy-id 913}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates With Circles and Spheres" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064393}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506663} :activity/id #uuid "2a8712d4-6984-598d-9326-16635a3b14bc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050411 :topic/id #uuid "46d83f8d-b8f7-5858-99d2-3e75f2b9bd8c" :topic/math-academy-id 287} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506604 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506590 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f09fc632-c551-511d-b5ff-0a2ef83dfc99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506354}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506354} :answer-field/id #uuid "70fdc4f0-4261-5d00-b2fa-9b5690965424" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d9d699d5-e68c-517c-8c66-c0218f7be321" :question/math-academy-id "q-344727" :question/problem "![](images/95/95e09441c0ab651a759862d3689aee517d089a43d2089697a1b77882de1e5d73.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a patio forms a circular pool. The radius of the pool increases at a rate of $3\\,\\text{m/min}.$ How fast is the area of the pool increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{5}{2\\pi }\\,\\text{m}?$\n\nThe area of the pool is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $r=\\frac{5}{2\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{5}{2\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $15\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} :step/id #uuid "04cf9317-baff-533e-bded-bc0975c757e1"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506534 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03e5c2fc-62c3-5cff-8c3f-12578ac7eec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505922} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd9e9ba-35f5-5dc8-b23d-1b83df5ca67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506037} {:answer/id #uuid "5ad7d797-78b2-528c-9d9b-806dc635caa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506068} {:answer/id #uuid "8c834b34-6910-5d98-ab6c-76660ea17f16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12.5\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506154} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ab0c31-9934-5df4-8de8-4658ed227b1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{in}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506207}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506207} :answer-field/id #uuid "893ce6e9-d2a5-5687-890a-43b20d335c93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3340fda4-31bb-56af-bac8-5dcbd67440f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116730" :question/problem "A spherical hot air balloon is inflated. At a particular moment, the rate of increase in the volume of the balloon is numerically equal to nine-halfs the rate of increase in its surface area. What is the radius of the balloon at that moment?" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =18\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & ={(18\\sqrt{\\pi })}^{2} \\\\ A & =324\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =324\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =81 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a9bc4ee9-11ca-5a48-8b4d-0b8a2d853ce7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506661}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506650 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94f771e8-4826-5b5d-8228-a990eba66454" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369205} {:answer/id #uuid "75a1d081-eea5-5784-82dd-15b7ad720ca4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.5\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369206} {:answer/id #uuid "4f9a3ece-f0fc-5902-8ec9-1de1e79a9224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369207} {:answer/id #uuid "fd7b5843-d568-544c-8d66-0fe759b8fdc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369208} {:answer/id #uuid "73d4ba9d-d85b-5d24-b9bf-734a0c435e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14\\text{ cm}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369209}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369206} :answer-field/id #uuid "2882600e-dd6b-59ca-8e2e-a290902aa712" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369204}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1d90b811-f213-55fb-a391-b4b1340be2c3" :question/math-academy-id "q-116647" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9a864a350b6493794639ea031c7d40242717f5e7325eb326228f82a9cb1c10c9.png)\n\nA graphic designer is animating a circle in such a way that the area of the circle increases at a rate of $7\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is the radius of the circle increasing at the moment when the radius is $1\\text{ cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=1,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=7\\pi $ and $r=1,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi (1) \\\\ 7\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =3.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius is increasing at a rate of $3.5$ centimeters per second."} :step/id #uuid "bdda543f-34f7-5aaf-9190-3fecab0d3532" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506661 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506535 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7482730a-2ef8-51f5-a35d-2f7208beda4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506120}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506120} :answer-field/id #uuid "22d1d24c-d1f3-5b29-bd9f-d25502738b67" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506393}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37194e35-397f-5f0f-b8ce-0e1f1331cb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344726" :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c16496b13f9ad9fc995cc59dedf1777f25e394ca61dd2ea493d8bd8f69c5e14.png)\n\nPaint dripping onto a floor forms a circular puddle. The radius of the puddle increases at a rate of $10\\,\\text{in/min}.$ How fast is the area of the puddle increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{9}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in}?$\n\nThe area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and we are also told that $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=10$ and $r=\\frac{9}{4\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =10\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{9}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the puddle is increasing at a rate of $45\\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} :step/id #uuid "f7e7553f-61e4-5efb-b004-ea189584c1c2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506604}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12cb41b0-01ea-5c46-86e5-04b8117b3dc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505946} {:answer/id #uuid "22fcb82c-2909-591f-be60-e9e88a12eb5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505967} {:answer/id #uuid "67a91bd3-65bd-58c2-86c6-7c75df5bee1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506087} {:answer/id #uuid "7d1966ac-a96b-5e2d-9d3c-63f6e79e8a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}\\,\\text{in}\\text{/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506125} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ad83a5-b560-5894-9b06-c5bb1a83e3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi \\,\\text{in/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506295}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186505946} :answer-field/id #uuid "a61e3d80-f1fd-5eef-8661-45e65506ae2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186506455}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e8399078-36ec-5b21-87c0-73ffc0d26600" :question/math-academy-id "q-2550" :question/problem "![](images/97/976db172587f6acdc7af6e426b01adf477e86ef731ce92939e122e291b53337b.png)\n\nA hot air balloon is inflated at the rate of $12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ How fast is the radius, $r$, changing at the moment when $r=2\\,\\text{in}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=12\\,{\\text{in}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=2\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=12$ and $r=2,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (2)^{2} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 16\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of the balloon is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{4\\pi }\\,\\text{in}/\\text{s}$ when the radius is $2\\,\\text{in}.$"} :step/id #uuid "fe01ad7d-1637-5e4b-88cb-7874304a764d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186506650}}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates With Circles and Spheres" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186505904}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521603} :activity/id #uuid "d4e9f94b-d25c-5269-b052-607fcefd509e" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050411 :topic/id #uuid "46d83f8d-b8f7-5858-99d2-3e75f2b9bd8c" :topic/math-academy-id 287} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4804743-754e-52a4-ac46-58a24afecd98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316482} :answer-field/id #uuid "87f30560-b7a8-5fe7-a866-713cb0a11c66" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a6cc0140-a8eb-5088-99b0-8b7a894e4ee5" :question/math-academy-id "q-344751" :question/problem "![](images/28/287add661cff085d1090295f67f87ee80eeaa3d8301ee6632ab54a812a6cd119.png)\n\nA spherical hailstone is melting uniformly, causing its radius to change at a rate of $\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s}.$ If the radius at a certain moment is $15\\,\\text{mm},$ what is the rate at which the surface area is decreasing?\n\nThe surface area of the hailstone is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone and $A$ its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\,\\text{mm}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=15\\,\\text{mm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the surface area formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}8\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }$ and $r=15.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{6\\pi }\\cdot 8\\pi (15) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the hailstone is decreasing at a rate of $20{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "16587f29-126f-56b6-8d74-cf78a419ed2b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521611}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f48b03f4-de1f-5518-8d7b-f0622fb8986b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296561} {:answer/id #uuid "8c96d101-996a-5077-8061-aff0a5c888fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521082} :answer-field/id #uuid "3697c49e-740a-5aaa-a707-4701abf7681d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296734}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cb5b4de1-d892-5870-99f2-1c26e26d0697" :question/math-academy-id "q-315860" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e850cd14f3b4f791c79c136d0fc799fd17fdc23eeab37e67ad2811f3ff7b6d0e.png)\n\nA snowball is melting in the sun and slowly shrinking. Determine the rate at which the surface area of the snowball is decreasing, given that the radius is decreasing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s}$ and its surface area at that moment is $100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} ${\\text{in}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the snowball and let $A$ be its surface area. Both $r$ and $A$ change with time. In particular, we are given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ when $A=100\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the surface area of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(4\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe then calculate the value of $r$ using $A=100\\pi .$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 100\\pi  & =4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 25 & ={r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\pm 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince radius cannot be negative, we have $r=5.$\n\nWe now substitute $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-\\frac{1}{5}$ and $r=5$ into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 8\\pi \\cdot 5 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the surface area of the snowball is decreasing at a rate of $8\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$"} :step/id #uuid "32bc3605-05ce-5789-aabb-0e63516ca447" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521624}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521603 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521456 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9aa873a6-fa87-5112-b328-59b790bb02cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "18\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521114} {:answer/id #uuid "a28cf962-96ea-5fd2-a648-e08c58efd301" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521129} {:answer/id #uuid "a6084d40-a60b-5595-86d6-684fff85cf4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521135} {:answer/id #uuid "a75bdbec-56f0-53b5-95b6-f2d3c36945c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521139} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3d86cc-339a-5c68-b539-6749321e41e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "24\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521288}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521135} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8c5f3c7-fd49-5b7c-9172-09702c7f16cd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ac5cd87-de15-5914-9f66-8d76ea6b7dee" :question/math-academy-id "q-2555" :question/problem "![](images/89/8946fc7f3b9a087304d893d69196f8462913974aab552b05a442a7460a619bef.png)\n\nWater leaking onto a floor forms a circular pool. The radius of the pool increases at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/min}.$ How fast is the area of the pool increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\,\\text{cm}?$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=6$ and $r=\\frac{2}{\\pi },$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =6\\cdot 2\\pi (\\frac{2}{\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the pool is increasing at a rate of $24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$"} :step/id #uuid "5dd093f9-e085-5318-9537-a6bfe287bc53" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521611 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521513 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae33c09f-da88-5496-ac0c-693ebc91e98f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521152}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521152} :answer-field/id #uuid "e74b5676-bbc7-5018-8226-6434bdbb2081" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521441}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "7842bd80-84fe-5d31-bbd1-38dac51d5756" :question/math-academy-id "q-344787" :question/problem "A spherical hailstone is growing as it moves through a storm cloud. At a particular moment, the rate of increase in the volume of the hailstone is numerically equal to three times the rate of increase in its surface area. What is the radius of the hailstone at that moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $r$ be the radius of the hailstone, $V$ the volume, and $A$ its surface area. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the volume $V$ and the surface area $A$ of the sphere. Since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2}$ and $V=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3},$ the relationship between $V$ and $A$ is\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{r}^{3}=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}{(\\frac{A}{4\\pi })}^{3/2}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{3(4\\pi )^{1/2}}=\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}A}(\\frac{{A}^{3/2}}{6\\sqrt{\\pi }}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{{A}^{1/2}}{4\\sqrt{\\pi }} \\\\ {A}^{1/2} & =12\\sqrt{\\pi } \\\\ A & =(12\\sqrt{\\pi })^{2} \\\\ A & =144\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $A=4\\pi {r}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\pi {r}^{2} & =144\\pi  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =36 \\\\ r & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6e4aa70b-d6b4-5bbf-8a4a-5884ae9c9244"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521552 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db110e87-ecff-5df6-86c8-2144e4e980f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521254}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521254} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c7ad603-536b-5d70-82a1-a923240f239d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d564be6e-5836-50c8-903c-6a70a3ceed94" :question/math-academy-id "q-344736" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bc7e248783ccf6b1eda95a1c2c80300e48d8cdb48f39ea07296ed3bf51bfaa29.png)\n\nA small, circular puddle evaporates on a floor. If the puddle’s area is decreasing at a rate of $32\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min},$ calculate the rate of change of its circumference at the moment when the radius is $8\\,\\text{cm}.$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe circumference of the puddle is decreasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}/\\text{min}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this situation, we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}$ at the moment when $r=8,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the circumference $C$ of the circle. Since $C=2\\pi r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $C$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{C}{2\\pi })}^{2}=\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}C}(\\frac{{C}^{2}}{4\\pi }) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{C}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $r=8\\,\\text{cm},$ $C=2\\pi (8)=16\\pi \\text{ cm}.$ Hence, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-32\\pi $ and $C=16\\pi ,$ and solving for $\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{16\\pi }{2\\pi } \\\\ -32\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 8 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}C}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the circumference is decreasing at a rate of $4\\pi \\text{ cm}\\text{/min}.$"} :step/id #uuid "98f6e106-e4e9-50d2-919d-6bd1471cddd5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521579}}] :activity/title "Calculating Related Rates With Circles and Spheres" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520756}]}
